320†pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL84: IUPAC Name  | disodium;selenate;decahydrate\nL85: SMILES  | O.O.O.O.O.O.O.O.O.O.[Na+].[Na+].[O-][Se]([O-])(=O)=O\nL86: ### Specifications\nL87: \nL88: Melting Point  | 35°C (decomposition)\nL89: Physical Form  | Crystalline\nL90: Quantity  | 100 g\nL91: Assay Percent Range  | 99.9% (metals basis)\nL92: UN Number  | UN2630\nL93: Merck Index  | 14,8671\nL94: Solubility Information  | Soluble in water.\nL95: Formula Weight  | 369.09 (188.94 Anhydrous)\nL96: Percent Purity  | 99.9%\nL97: Density  | 1.600 g/mL\nL98: Show More  |\nL99: ## 【0†Safety and Handling】\nL100: \nL101: Hazard Category  | H301+H331-H373\nL102: Hazard Statement  | GHS H Statement\nL103: H301-H331-H373\nL104: Toxic if swallowed.\nL105: Toxic if inhaled.\nL106: May cause damage to organs through prolonged or repeated exposure.\nL107: Precautionary Statement  | P260-P264b-P270-P271-P301+P310-P304+P340-P311-P314-P330-P501c\nL108: DOTInformation  | Transport Hazard Class: 6.1; Packing Group: I; Proper Shipping Name: SELENATES\nL109: TSCA  | Yes\nL110: Recommended Storage  | Ambient temperatures\nL111: \nL112: RUO – Research Use Only\nL113: \nL114: Product Title\nL115: Type of Issue *\nL116: \nL117: [Select]\nL118: \nL119: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL120: \nL121: By clicking Submit, you acknowledge that you may be contacted by Fisher Scientific in regards to the feedback you have provided in this form. We will not share your information for any other purposes. All contact information provided shall also be maintained in accordance with our 【12†Privacy Policy†www.thermofisher.com】.\nL122: \nL123: Spot an opportunity for improvement?\nL124: \nL125: [Input]\nL126: \nL127: * * *\nL128: ### About Us\nL129: \nL130: 【13†Fisher Scientific】\nL131: \nL132: 【14†All Brands】\nL133: \nL134: 【15†Quality Management】\nL135: \nL136: 【16†Thermo Fisher Scientific†corporate.thermofisher.com】\nL137: \nL138: 【17†Policies and Notices†www.thermofisher.com】\nL139: \nL140: ### Programs and Services\nL141: \nL142: 【18†Encompass Procurement Services】\nL143: \nL144: 【19†eProcurement Integration Solutions】\nL145: \nL146: 【20†Government Contract Resource Center】\nL147: \nL148: 【21†Small and Specialty Supplier Program】\nL149: \nL150: 【22†Sustainability Program】\nL151: \nL152: 【23†Thermo Fisher Financial Services】\nL153: \nL154: 【24†Unity Lab Services】\nL155: ### Customer Support\nL156: \nL157: 【25†Help & Support Center】\nL158: \nL159: 【26†Account Dashboard】\nL160: \nL161: 【1†Create an Account】\nL162: \nL163: 【27†Order Status】\nL164: \nL165: 【28†Quick Order】\nL166: \nL167: Report a Site Issue\nL168: \nL169: 【29†Contact Us】\nL170: ### Connect with Us\nL171: \nL172: 【30†Lab Reporter】\nL173: \nL174: 【31†Sign Up for Emails】\nL175: \nL176: 【32†Image: Fisher Scientific - part of Thermo Fisher Scientific】\nL177: We keep science moving forward by offering over 4 million products and extensive support services to the research, production, healthcare, and science education markets. Count on us for an unrivaled selection of lab, life sciences, safety, and facility management supplies—including chemicals, equipment, instruments, diagnostics, and much more—along with exceptional customer care from an industry-leading team that’s proud to be part of Thermo Fisher Scientific.\nL178: © 2026 Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc. All rights reserved.\nL179: All trademarks are the property of Thermo Fisher Scientific and its subsidiaries unless otherwise specified.\nL180: \nL181: 【33†Trademarks】\nL182: \nL183: 【12†Privacy Notice†www.thermofisher.com】\nL184: \nL185: 【34†Terms & Conditions】\nL186: \nL187: 【35†Return Policy】\nL188: \nL189: © 2026 Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc. All rights reserved.\nL190: All trademarks are the property of Thermo Fisher Scientific and its subsidiaries unless otherwise specified.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(IUCr) Crystal structures of Na2SeO4·1.5H2O and Na2SeO4·10H2O (https://journals.iucr.org/e/issues/2014/08/00/hb0010/)\nciteturn47view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.iucr.org/e/issues/2014/08/00/hb0010/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 650\nL0:  | Acta Crystallographica Section E\nL1: Acta Crystallographica\nL2: Section E CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC COMMUNICATIONS\nL3: \nL4: 【0†IUCr†www.iucr.org】 【1†IT†it.iucr.org】 【2†WDC†wdc.iucr.org】\nL5: \nL6: search IUCr Journals[Input] [Input]\nL7: \nL8: 【3†home】 【4†archive】 【5†editors】 【6†for authors】 【7†for readers】 【8†submit】 【9†open access】\nL9: \nL10: 【3†home】 【4†archive】 【5†editors】 【6†for authors】 【7†for readers】 【8†submit】 【9†open access】\nL11: \nL12: 【10†Image: Navigation】\nL13: \nL14: 【10†Image: Navigation】\nL15: ## research communications\\(\\def\\hfill{\\hskip 5em}\\def\\hfil{\\hskip 3em}\\def\\eqno#1{\\hfil {#1}}\\)\nL16: \nL17:  | CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC\nL18: COMMUNICATIONS\nL19: \nL20: ISSN: 2056-9890\nL21: \nL22: 【4†Volume 70】| 【11†Part 8】| 【11†August 2014】| Pages 54-57\nL23: \nL24: 【12†doi:10.1107/S1600536814011799†dx.doi.org】\nL25: \nL26: Open 【13†Image: Open Access】 access\nL27: ## Crystal structures of Na_{2}SeO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O and Na_{2}SeO_{4}·10H_{2}O\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Image†crossmark-cdn.crossref.org】\nL30: \nL31: 【15†Matthias Weil†scripts.iucr.org】【16†^{a}】【17†^{*}】 and 【18†Barbara Bonneau†scripts.iucr.org】【19†^{b}】\nL32: \nL33: ^{a}Institute for Chemical Technologies and Analytics, Division of Structural Chemistry, Vienna University of Technology, Getreidemarkt 9/164-SC, A-1060 Vienna, Austria, and ^{b}IUT Bordeaux 1, 15 Rue Naudet, 33175 Gradignan, France\nL34: ^{*}Correspondence e-mail: 【20†[email protected]】\nL35: Edited by W. T. A. Harrison, University of Aberdeen, Scotland (Received 2 May 2014; accepted 21 May 2014; online 19 July 2014)\nL36: The crystal structures of Na_{2}SeO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O (sodium selenate sesquihydrate) and Na_{2}SeO_{4}·10H_{2}O (sodium selenate deca­hydrate) are isotypic with those of Na_{2}CrO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O and Na_{2}X SeO_{4}·10H_{2}O (X = S, Cr), respectively. The 【21†asymmetric unit†dictionary.iucr.org】 of the sesquihydrate contains two Na^{+} cations, one SeO_{4} tetra­hedron and one and a half water mol­ecules, the other half being generated by twofold rotation symmetry.\nL37: The coordination polyhedra of the cations are a distorted monocapped octa­hedron and a square pyramid; these [NaO_{x}] polyhedra are linked through common edges and corners into a three-dimensional framework structure, the voids of which are filled with the Se atoms of the SeO_{4} tetra­hedra. The structure is consolidated by O—H⋯O hydrogen bonds between coordinating water mol­ecules and framework O atoms.\nL38: The 【21†asymmetric unit†dictionary.iucr.org】 of the deca­hydrate consists of two Na^{+} cations, one SeO_{4} tetra­hedron and ten water mol­ecules. Both Na^{+} cations are octa­hedrally surrounded by water mol­ecules and by edge-sharing condensed into zigzag chains extending parallel to [001]. The SeO_{4} tetra­hedra and two uncoordinating water mol­ecules are situated between the chains and are connected to the chains through an intricate network of medium-strength O—H⋯O hydrogen bonds.\nL39: Keywords: 【22†isotypism†scripts.iucr.org】; 【23†sodium selenate†scripts.iucr.org】; 【24†salt hydrates†scripts.iucr.org】; 【25†crystal structure†scripts.iucr.org】.\nL40: \nL41: CCDC references: 【26†1004274†scripts.iucr.org】; 【27†1004275†scripts.iucr.org】\nL42: ### 1. Chemical context\nL43: Based on recent studies in the system Na/Se/O/H that revealed dimorphism of the phases NaHSeO_{4} and Na_{5}H_{3}(SeO_{4})_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2} (Pollitt & Weil, 2014), we became inter­ested in the structure determination of hydrous phases of Na_{2}SeO_{4}. Although the first report of the deca­hydrate of Na_{2}SeO_{4} dates back to 1827 (Mitscherlich, 1827), a detailed structure report for this compound has not been published so far.\nL44: Mitscherlich (1827) also recognized an isomorphic relationship of Na_{2}SeO_{4}·10H_{2}O with Na_{2}SO_{4}·10H_{2}O (Glauber's salt or mirabilite as a mineral species). This relation was later confirmed by Rosický (1908) and by Ruben et al. (1961) on the basis of unit-cell determinations using diffraction methods.\nL45: Another hydrous phase of Na_{2}SeO_{4} reported in the literature is the metastable hepta­hydrate that crystallized from supersaturated Na_{2}SeO_{4} solutions only when seeded with Na_{2}SO_{4}·7H_{2}O nuclei below 293 K (Belarew, 1965).\nL46: During crystallization studies of aqueous Na_{2}SeO_{4} solutions under different temperature conditions, we were able to isolate crystals not only of the deca­hydrate, but also of the sesqui­hydrate, the crystal structures of which are reported here.\nL47: ### 2. Structural commentary\nL48: Na_{2}SeO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O is isotypic with the corresponding chromate (Kahlenberg, 2012) and is the second example of the Na_{2}X O_{4}·1.5H_{2}O structure family. The main building blocks of this structure type are distorted [NaO_{5}(H_{2}O)_{2}] (Na1) monocapped octa­hedra, distorted [NaO_{4}(H_{2}O)] square pyramids (Na2) (Fig. 1) and rather regular X O_{4} (X = Se, Cr) tetra­hedra. These building blocks are linked through common corners and edges into a three-dimensional framework structure (Fig. 2).\nL49: Hydrogen bonds of the type O—H⋯O between the coord­in­ating water mol­ecules and parts of the framework O atoms provide additional stabilization (Table 1). The bond lengths (Table 2) and angles within the individual building blocks of the selenate and chromate structures are more or less identical with mean distances of SeO_{4} = 1.641; CrO_{4} = 1.651; Na1O_{7} = 2.514 (selenate), 2.505 (chromate); Na2O_{5} = 2.350 (selenate), 2.360 Å (chromate).\nL50: Table 1\nL51: Hydrogen-bond geometry (Å, °) for 1.5-hydrate\nL52: D—H⋯A D—H H⋯A D⋯A D—H⋯A\nL53: O W 5—H1⋯O4^{viii} 0.82 (1) 2.13 (1) 2.922 (2) 164 (3)\nL54: O W 5—H2⋯O3^{ix} 0.82 (1) 2.08 (1) 2.891 (2) 169 (3)\nL55: O W 6—H3⋯O1^{vi} 0.82 (1) 1.90 (1) 2.703 (2) 167 (4)\nL56: Symmetry codes: (vi) 【28†Image: [x, -y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (viii) 【29†Image: [x+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z]】; (ix) 【30†Image: [x+{\\script{3\\over 4}}, y-{\\script{1\\over 4}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 4}}]】.\nL57: Table 2\nL58: Selected bond lengths (Å) for 1.5-hydrate\nL59: Na1—O W 5 2.3660 (18) Na2—O2^{iv} 2.3301 (18)\nL60: Na1—O3^{i} 2.4157 (19) Na2—O W 6^{v} 2.3480 (18)\nL61: Na1—O1 2.4379 (18) Na2—O4^{vi} 2.3651 (19)\nL62: Na1—O3^{ii} 2.4594 (16) Na2—O1^{vii} 2.4103 (18)\nL63: Na1—O W 5^{i} 2.465 (2) Se1—O2 1.6350 (14)\nL64: Na1—O4^{iii} 2.6057 (19) Se1—O3 1.6367 (14)\nL65: Na1—O2^{ii} 2.8475 (17) Se1—O4 1.6451 (16)\nL66: Na2—O2 2.298 (2) Se1—O1 1.6481 (15)\nL67: Symmetry codes: (i) 【31†Image: [x+{\\script{1\\over 4}}, y+{\\script{1\\over 4}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 4}}]】; (ii) 【32†Image: [x+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z]】; (iii) x+1, y, z; (iv) 【33†Image: [x-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -y, -z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (v) x-1, y, z; (vi) 【28†Image: [x, -y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (vii) 【34†Image: [x-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z]】.\nL68:  | 【35†Figure 1】\nL69: The NaO_{7} and NaO_{5} polyhedra in the structure of Na_{2}SO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O. Displacement parameters are drawn at the 99% probability level.\nL70: [Symmetry codes: (i) 【36†Image: [x+{\\script{1\\over 4}}]】, 【37†Image: [y+{\\script{1\\over 4}}]】, 【38†Image: [-z+{\\script{1\\over 4}}]】; (ii) 【39†Image: [x+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, 【40†Image: [y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, z; (iii) x+1, y, z; (iv) 【41†Image: [x-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, -y, 【42†Image: [-z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (v) x-1, y, z; (vi) x, 【43†Image: [-y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, 【42†Image: [-z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (vii) 【41†Image: [x-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, 【44†Image: [y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】, z.]\nL71:  | 【45†Figure 2】\nL72: The crystal structure of Na_{2}SO_{4}·1.5H_{2}O in a projection along [110]. NaO_{5} polyhedra are turquoise, NaO_{7} polyhedra are blue, SeO_{4} tetra­hedra are red and H atoms are grey. Hydrogen bonds have been omitted for clarity.\nL73: Isotypism has been reported for several Na_{2}X O_{4}·10H_{2}O (X = S, Se, Cr, W, Mo) phases (Ruben et al., 1961), but only the structures of X = S (Levy & Lisensky, 1978; Prescott et al., 2001) and Cr (Kahlenberg, 2012) have been determined so far. As expected, the general structural set-up in the isotypic Na_{2}X O_{4}·10H_{2}O structures is very similar.\nL74: Each of the two Na^{+} cations is octa­hedrally surrounded [mean Na—O distance of the two octa­hedra is 2.420 Å (see Table 3); sulfate analogue (Prescott et al., 2001): 2.415 Å; chromate analogue (Kahlenberg, 2012): 2.423 Å]. The [NaO_{6}] octa­hedra are linked via edge-sharing into zigzag chains (Fig. 3) running parallel to [001].\nL75: These chains are linked with neighbouring chains and inter­mediate SeO_{4} tetra­hedra (mean Se—O distance 1.639; sulfate 1.488, chromate 1.647 Å) and non-coordinating lattice water mol­ecules through O—H⋯O hydrogen bonds of medium strength (Table 4) to build up the crystal structure (Fig. 4).\nL76: The most important difference between the structures of the three Na_{2}X O_{4}·10H_{2}O (X = S, Se, Cr) phases is the missing disorder of the X O_{4} tetra­hedron in the selenate compound that has been observed in the sulfate compound on the basis of single-crystal neutron data (Levy & Lisensky, 1978) and single-crystal X-ray data (Prescott et al., 2001), or for the chromate compound on the basis of single-crystal X-ray data (Kahlenberg, 2012).\nL77: Table 3\nL78: Selected bond lengths (Å) for 10-hydrate\nL79: Na1—O W 5 2.3776 (6) Na2—O W 7 2.3935 (6)\nL80: Na1—O W 6^{i} 2.4181 (6) Na2—O W 9 2.4325 (6)\nL81: Na1—O W 11 2.4184 (6) Na2—O W 6 2.4415 (6)\nL82: Na1—O W 10 2.4194 (6) Na2—O W 10^{ii} 2.4667 (6)\nL83: Na1—O W 8^{i} 2.4473 (6) Se1—O41 1.6335 (5)\nL84: Na1—O W 9^{i} 2.4507 (6) Se1—O31 1.6394 (5)\nL85: Na2—O W 12 2.3814 (6) Se1—O1 1.6398 (5)\nL86: Na2—O W 5^{ii} 2.3891 (6) Se1—O21 1.6421 (5)\nL87: Symmetry codes: (i) x, y, z+1; (ii) 【46†Image: [x, -y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】.\nL88: Table 4\nL89: Hydrogen-bond geometry (Å, °) for 10-hydrate\nL90: D—H⋯A D—H H⋯A D⋯A D—H⋯A\nL91: O W 5—H5 A⋯O41 0.82 (1) 1.96 (1) 2.7570 (7) 164 (1)\nL92: O W 5—H5 B⋯O W 13^{iii} 0.82 (1) 2.00 (1) 2.7980 (7) 165 (1)\nL93: O W 6—H6 A⋯O W 14 0.82 (1) 2.02 (1) 2.8301 (7) 168 (1)\nL94: O W 6—H6 B⋯O41^{ii} 0.82 (1) 1.98 (1) 2.7791 (7) 166 (2)\nL95: O W 7—H7 A⋯O1^{iv} 0.82 (1) 1.97 (1) 2.7727 (7) 166 (1)\nL96: O W 7—H7 B⋯O W 8^{v} 0.82 (1) 1.95 (1) 2.7542 (7) 168 (1)\nL97: O W 8—H8 A⋯O41^{ii} 0.82 (1) 1.95 (1) 2.7544 (7) 166 (1)\nL98: O W 8—H8 B⋯O W 7^{vi} 0.82 (1) 1.99 (1) 2.8076 (7) 178 (1)\nL99: O W 9—H9 A⋯O1^{vii} 0.82 (1) 2.11 (1) 2.9152 (7) 168 (1)\nL100: O W 9—H9 B⋯O W 13^{viii} 0.82 (1) 2.04 (1) 2.8596 (7) 177 (1)\nL101: O W 10—H10 A⋯O W 14^{ix} 0.82 (1) 2.05 (1) 2.8686 (7) 178 (2)\nL102: O W 10—H10 B⋯O31^{x} 0.82 (1) 2.08 (1) 2.8920 (7) 174 (1)\nL103: O W 11—H11 A⋯O31 0.82 (1) 2.05 (1) 2.8604 (7) 171 (1)\nL104: O W 11—H11 B⋯O W 12^{i} 0.82 (1) 1.96 (1) 2.7716 (8) 168 (1)\nL105: O W 12—H12 A⋯O21^{iv} 0.82 (1) 1.92 (1) 2.7359 (7) 179 (1)\nL106: O W 12—H12 B⋯O W 11^{viii} 0.82 (1) 1.97 (1) 2.7818 (7) 173 (1)\nL107: O W 13—H13 A⋯O1^{xi} 0.82 (1) 1.98 (1) 2.7932 (7) 172 (1)\nL108: O W 13—H13 B⋯O21^{x} 0.82 (1) 1.98 (1) 2.7931 (7) 170 (1)\nL109: O W 14—H14 A⋯O21^{xii} 0.82 (1) 1.98 (1) 2.8002 (7) 174 (1)\nL110: O W 14—H14 B⋯O31^{viii} 0.82 (1) 2.00 (1) 2.8061 (7) 169 (1)\nL111: Symmetry codes: (i) x, y, z+1; (ii) 【46†Image: [x, -y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (iii) x+1, y, z; (iv) 【47†Image: [x, -y+{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z-{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (v) 【48†Image: [x, -y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (vi) 【49†Image: [-x+2, y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】; (vii) -x+2, -y, -z+1; (viii) -x+1, -y, -z+1; (ix) -x+1, -y-1, -z+1; (x) 【50†Image: [-x+1, y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -z+{\\script{3\\over 2}}]】; (xi) -x+1, -y, -z+2; (xii) 【51†Image: [-x+1, y-{\\script{1\\over 2}}, -z+{\\script{1\\over 2}}]】.\nL112:  | 【52†Figure 3】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn47view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/search?q=sodium%20selenate%20decahydrate&ontology=chebi&rows=20\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/search?ontology=chebi&q=sodium+selenate+decahydrate&rows=20 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn47view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:77775\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI%3A77775 is not accessible via this tool."
